WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.230
The solar street light outside our

2
00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:04.650
school comes on by itself at dusk.

3
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.660
Nobody presses a switch.

4
00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:08.810
So who decides?

5
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:12.380
A tiny computer, smaller

6
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:13.640
than your fingernail.

7
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.100
Today: meet the microcontroller,

8
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:18.860
and write your first program.

9
00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:22.580
A microcontroller is a whole small

10
00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.960
computer on one chip: a processor, a

11
00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:28.600
little memory, and pins, metal legs

12
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:29.940
that connect it to the world.

13
00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.610
It runs one program all day,

14
00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:34.230
on very little power.

15
00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:37.830
You'll find them everywhere: in TV

16
00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:40.750
remotes, washing machines, prepaid

17
00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:42.610
electricity meters and cars.

18
00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:46.230
Boards like the micro bit, Arduino and

19
00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:49.150
ESP32 put one where you can program it.

20
00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:53.150
Each pin is an input or an output.

21
00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:55.970
Inputs bring the world in:

22
00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:57.850
buttons and sensors.

23
00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:02.210
Outputs act: LEDs, buzzers and motors.

24
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.400
The micro bit even has a light sensor

25
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.880
and twenty-five LEDs built in.

26
00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:12.070
A microcontroller can't see light: it

27
00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:14.370
reads the sensor as a number, made of

28
00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:14.610
bits.

29
00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:18.200
The micro bit uses one byte: zero

30
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.520
for dark, up to two hundred and

31
00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:21.820
fifty-five for bright.

32
00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:26.270
An Arduino Uno uses ten bits, up to

33
00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:27.750
one thousand and twenty-three.

34
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:29.380
Quick check!

35
00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.200
A micro bit's light reading drops

36
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.300
from two hundred to twenty.

37
00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.220
Did it get darker or brighter?

38
00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:39.190
Darker.

39
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.220
Maybe a cloud passed, or a

40
00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:43.340
hand covered the sensor.

41
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.980
The microcontroller only knows

42
00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:47.160
the number went down.

43
00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.290
What it does next is up to your program.

44
00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:53.250
Every program sets up once.

45
00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.600
Then it loops forever: read the sensor,

46
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.000
decide, act, wait a moment, and go round

47
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.020
again, many times a second.

48
00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:04.980
Here's a night light.

49
00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:07.880
Forever: read the light level.

50
00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.720
If it's below fifty, switch the LED on.

51
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:13.940
Otherwise, switch it off.

52
00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:16.720
Then wait a moment, and repeat.

53
00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:20.350
Build it as blocks in MakeCode, free

54
00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:22.210
in a browser, and test it on the

55
00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:23.890
simulator, with no board at all.

56
00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.090
Or draw it on paper as a

57
00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:28.210
flowchart: a diamond for each

58
00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:30.310
question, a box for each action.

59
00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:32.060
Your turn.

60
00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:35.270
Tunde's night light has no forever loop:

61
00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:37.890
it reads the light once, at the start.

62
00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.360
He switches it on in the afternoon.

63
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:42.960
What happens when night falls?

64
00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.550
Nothing: without the loop, it

65
00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:47.850
read once and stopped.

66
00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.390
Remember: a microcontroller is a computer

67
00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:54.310
on a chip, inputs bring the world in,

68
00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:57.970
outputs act, and the program reads,

69
00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:01.030
decides and acts, again and again.

70
00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:04.170
Try it yourself: draw your

71
00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.850
night light as a flowchart.

72
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:09.500
Then build it in MakeCode, and slide the

73
00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:11.780
simulator's light level up and down.

74
00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:13.280
Got a micro bit?

75
00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:16.670
Cover it with your hand and watch, then

76
00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:19.010
change fifty to suit your room.
